  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 133 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nThe upper and surface low located over Minnesota and Wisconsin will \ncontinue to occlude while slowly moving east through the remainder \nof this afternoon and evening. To the northwest we see an upper\nridge and surface high pressure building in. Much drier air \nthrough the mid-levels will work into the region, ending light \nrain/drizzle from northwest to southeast through the overnight. \nAll precipitation should be ended/east of the region by daybreak\nThursday. Overnight and through most of the day Thursday \nnorthwest winds remain breezy with widespread gusts of 15-25 \nmph. Occasional gusts up to around 30 mph are possible along \nthe Buffalo Ridge. Mixy winds keep overnight lows a bit warmer \nthan they would otherwise be. However, they are still expected \nto be colder than we've seen recently, in the low to mid 30s, \nwarmest over northwest Iowa. \n\nBy late Thursday afternoon the upper ridge axis will cross \nthrough the region. Pressure gradients will relax and winds will\ntaper off from the late afternoon and through the evening. \nThough dry air will be present in the mid-levels, a pesky thin \nlayer of moisture just above the boundary layer will keep broken\nclouds in our forecast through the remainder of Thursday. This \nalong with weak CAA will limit highs to the low to mid 40s. \nOvernight winds become light and variable while clouds slowly \nbreak up. Lows will drop into the mid 20s to low 30s by daybreak\nFriday. For many, this will be the first hard freeze of the \nseason. \n\nFriday clouds will finally break up and become mostly sunny in the \nafternoon. The previously mentioned surface high will continue to \nmove east, turning light winds to the south. Highs for Friday will \nbe seasonably cool in the 40s, with overnight lows falling to the \nmid 20s to low 30s. A series of upper waves will pass through the \nregion Saturday into early next week. Mid-levels continue to be very \ndry, so not expecting precipitation with these. However, a few low-\nlevel clouds may be possible. Highs during this period will warm \nfrom the 50s Saturday, to low 60s Sunday, to mid 60s Monday. Lows \nwill generally be in the 30s.",
